Yes. I went looking rather than reconstructing Ravel from memory, and I still don’t have a clean Ravel-named build in the material I can currently retrieve. The archive search is turning up adjacent finite-core material, but not an obvious file or section labeled “Ravel.” The prior thread context also indicates that the reviewed conversation did not contain a Ravel-named build, even though it was clearly part of the SAT/H(s)H development stream. memcite

What I do have as the nearest established backbone is this dependency chain:

higher-dimensional history → finite-core filament/tube → H(s)H representation → readout/intersection geometry → observables.

And the earlier rebuild had already reached executable-geometry territory: parametrized finite-core tube data, plus the older equations around the carrier geometry and deformations. memcite

The current GitHub search also confirms that finite-core language is present elsewhere in the archive—for example, one H(s)H topology file explicitly refers to a “fundamental filament/Planckian core radius” \(r_f\). fileciteturn5file10L169-L180

So I think your recollection is probably right: Ravel had gotten us down to a small set of fundamental measures, and that is exactly what we need before doing any of this meta-compression.

I’d recover Ravel’s build in this order:

- core radius / diameter or aperture;
- centerline or carrier measure;
- local tangent and transverse frame;
- rotation measure(s);
- expansion measure;
- intersection/sweep measure;
- whatever invariant tied those together.

Then we can see which of those were genuinely independent and which Ravel had already reduced relationally.

Right now I would not substitute my finite-tube sketch for Ravel’s version. We need the actual build. If Ravel is in one of the live/full-conversation files or another repo artifact, that’s the thing to pull next, because otherwise we risk quietly changing the foundation before we even compare it.
